WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:02.000
He's brave.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.000
He's relatable.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:03.600
He's funny.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.080
He's friendly, spectacular, and amazing.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:10.120
But Spider-Man can also be unprofessional,
and maybe a little pervy.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.280
If you want to keep your childhood memories
of Spidey intact, bail out now.

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:18.620
But if you want to see Spider-Man for who
he really is, warts and all, be warned: without

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.520
the veneer of nostalgia, you might not like
what you see.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:22.800
Fake news

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:28.620
Peter Parker, through his work with the Daily
Bugle, is a Pulitzer Prize-winning photojournalist,

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.880
and that means that he's expected to adhere
to certain ethical standards.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:33.380
He doesn't.

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:36.720
In fact, his entire career is pretty much
a lie.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.870
While Peter tells J. Jonah Jameson that his
photos of Spider-Man swinging through the

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:44.730
city are candid, he often stages shots in
order to get the best angle.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:47.960
In reality, he's shooting selfies and selling
them as news.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:52.980
He creates newsworthy events, and then uses
them to his financial advantage, which is

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:53.940
borderline evil.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:59.320
Peter's transgressions are serious enough
that he could be legally liable for "misrepresentation,

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:03.780
fraud, and breach of contract," and he wouldn't
have much of a defense to fall back on.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:08.300
In other words, Peter Parker is a hack who
shouldn't be allowed anywhere near a camera,

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.100
and should never be trusted with the news.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:11.760
And speaking of cameras…

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:13.880
Mary Jane's indiscretions

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.740
It's not just fake news that Peter's cameras
get used for.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:21.250
He also spends a lot of time focusing on his
wife....

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:24.620
Mostly when she's naked, which you may not
completely realize as a kid.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:30.620
In Spectacular Spider-Man #134, Mary Jane
takes some sexy shots to convince Peter to

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:35.370
become a fashion photographer, while Peter
crawls on the wall and pulls her to the ceiling

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.240
for some adult recreation.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:42.380
In The Amazing Spider-Man #300, Peter and
MJ break in their new apartment and relieve

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:46.780
some Venom-induced stress by taking some snaps
for their "private collection."

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:52.140
In The Sensational Spider-Man #20, Peter takes
his mind off a string of recent failures by

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:55.260
putting his "photographic talents" to use
in the bedroom.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:59.710
We just can't figure out why Mary Jane would
be so eager to jump back in front of the camera

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.380
during her time off from being a model.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:02.240
Get a hobby, lady.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:04.100
Spider-nonsense

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.460
Peter Parker was given spider-like powers
by a radioactive spider bite, and for the

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:10.820
most part, it checks out.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:12.080
Clinging to walls?

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:12.760
Sure.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:13.520
Super-strength?

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:14.260
Absolutely.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.100
Jumping extra high?

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:19.520
According to science, Spider-Man doesn't even
jump high enough.

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:23.520
So where the heck did his famous spider-sense
powers come from?

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:27.700
Spiders can sense movement in the air around
them using small hairs on their bodies, and

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:32.260
they can track the vibrations in their webs
to tell different types of insects apart.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:36.379
That means that Spider-Man should be able
to identify nearby physical threats… but

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:38.980
his spider-sense does much, much more than
that.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:42.680
"My spider-senses are starting to tingle!"

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.480
It tells Peter when an enemy is in disguise,
or if someone is lying.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:47.960
It guides him to hidden threats.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:52.280
It picks up specific radio frequencies, which
is how his spider-tracers work.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:57.240
Spider-sense is less of a biological function
and more of a psychic power, and it's not

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:59.040
anything like a real spider's abilities.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:00.380
Not even close.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:01.900
With great power...

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:08.120
"Remember, with great power, comes great responsibility."

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.160
Except that's not actually how it goes.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:17.280
In Amazing Fantasy #15, the actual quote reads,
"With great power there must also come — great

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:18.320
responsibility!"

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:23.159
That doesn't flow off the tongue quite as
well as the abbreviated version, so it makes

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:25.120
sense that future writers would try and condense
it a little bit.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:30.660
It's also a quote commonly attributed to Uncle
Ben, but Ben never actually says those words

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.660
in Amazing Fantasy #15, Spidey's first appearance.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.440
During Spider-Man's origin story, Uncle Ben
only has two lines: he wakes Peter up for

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:44.290
school, and comments on how Peter is getting
too big to out-wrestle thanks to Aunt May's

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:45.500
fat-filled wheatcakes.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:52.020
The quote by which Spider-Man lives his life
is only spoken by an unnamed narrator, who

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:56.640
utters the line as Spider-Man tearfully wanders
off into the night, still mourning his uncle's

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:57.140
death.

00:03:57.160 --> 00:03:59.180
Your whole life is a lie, Spider-Man!

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:00.700
Joke's on Carnage

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:04.500
Carnage might be the coolest Spider-Man villain
ever.

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:08.600
He's like Venom, but faster, stronger, and
looks like he's made of meat.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:11.280
He's more than a match for most Marvel heroes.

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:16.320
He has complete control over his symbiote,
and eats other symbiotes in order to boost

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:17.100
his strength.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:18.640
What more could you want in a bad guy?

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:20.800
Well, how about some originality?

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:24.860
For starters, he's basically a super-sized
version of Venom, who's already basically

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:26.720
just "Spider-Man, but evil."

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:29.100
The rip-off report is only getting started,
though.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:34.800
In his civilian identity of Cletus Kasady,
he closely resembles yet another famous character.

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:41.200
If the maniacal grin, sharp cheekbones, and
the psychotic disregard for human life remind

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:43.840
you of a clown-themed villain, you're not
alone.

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:50.440
In 2007, Carnage co-creator Erik Larsen flat-out
admitted that Cletus Kasady is a straight-up

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:51.460
Joker knockoff, saying,

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:53.740
"He was inspired by the Joker.

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:58.660
I basically drew the Joker and had him colored
with regular skin and red hair."

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:02.780
Larsen isn't the only one who thought Joker
and Carnage shared some strong similarities,

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:03.320
either.

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:10.180
In 1995, the Spider-Man and Batman crossover
comic saw the Clown Prince of Crime team up

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:15.120
with the symbiote to wreak havoc in Gotham
City, bringing the sinister twins together

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:15.620
at last.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:17.880
Spider-Man's terrible friends

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:23.700
Many heroes have personal ties to their arch-nemeses,
but Spider-Man takes things to a whole new

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:24.200
level.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:29.140
Basically, anyone in Peter Parker's life who
isn't dead turns into a supervillain sooner

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:29.640
or later.

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:30.760
No, seriously.

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:36.520
There's Norman Osborn, of course, who happens
to be both his best friend's dad and the evil

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:37.420
Green Goblin.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:42.420
Once Norman's out of the picture, his son
Harry picks up the pumpkin bombs and carries

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:43.420
on the family legacy.

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:47.720
The clone-obsessed Jackal is Peter's college
professor, Miles Warren.

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:53.040
The Molten Man is the stepbrother of Peter's
high school crush, Liz Allan.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:58.880
Man-Wolf is Peter's boss' son, while J. Jonah
Jameson himself funded the creation of the

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:02.000
Spider-Slayers, as well as the villainous
Scorpion.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:06.960
Ned Leeds, one of Peter's co-workers at the
Daily Bugle, spent some time as the Hobgoblin,

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:07.940
just for fun.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:09.520
And those are just the classics.

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:15.060
More recently, a good friend of Aunt May's,
the philanthropist Martin Li, turned out to

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:16.240
be the evil Mr. Negative.

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:22.500
Harry Osborn's fiancée, Lily Hollister, reveals
herself as the goblin-themed villain Menace.

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:28.680
And Phil Urich, the nephew of Peter's Daily
Bugle pal, Ben Urich, decided to terrorize

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:30.880
the city as yet another Hobgoblin.

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:34.340
They say you can't choose your family, but
you can choose your friends.

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:37.320
If that's really true, Peter, choose better.

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:38.440
These people suck.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:39.520
Thanks for watching!

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:41.840
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:44.100
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

